The baseline `test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }}` is a self-contained copy of a kernel
that lives in `{{ kernel_repo }}`. The winner `output/{{ kernel_name }}_optimized{{ ext }}`
is that copy after the trial loop improved it. Your job is to carry the improvement back to
the original files, as a patch, and nothing more.

## Inputs

- `{{ profile_path }}` -- which repository, which commit (HEAD), and the file:line of every
  region the baseline was copied from.
- `test_kernels/{{ kernel_name }}{{ ext }}` and `output/{{ kernel_name }}_optimized{{ ext }}`
  -- diff them; that diff is the change to port.
- `trials/{{ kernel_name }}/state.json` -- the best trial's measured speedup and strategy.

If `output/` holds no winner, stop: there is nothing to port.

## Rules

1. **Never modify `{{ kernel_repo }}`.** Work in a private clone inside this workspace:
   `git clone --shared --no-checkout <repo> upstream && git -C upstream checkout <HEAD from the profile>`.
   `{{ kernel_repo }}` stays read-only; the porting edits go into the private clone's
   working tree (`upstream/`) and nowhere else.
2. **Change what the winner changed, nowhere else.** Map each hunk of the baseline-to-winner
   diff onto the original file and region the profile names. Add a file only when the kernel
   cannot be expressed without one. Add no fallback, flag or environment switch.
3. **Keep the repository's conventions**: its formatting, its launch-configuration style,
   its build files. If the winner needs a compile flag the build does not pass, change the
   build target the profile names, and only that.
4. **Every win ports. Try, in this order, and stop at the first that applies:**
   1. **upstream** -- the winner keeps the op's schema, signature, bindings and every call
      site, and needs no dispatch by shape. A different algorithm, or fused or eliminated
      launches, inside the op is in this case: replace the original implementation with the
      winner's in the same file, behind the same entry point.
      `xe-forge-skill upstream clone {{ kernel_repo }}`, edit `upstream/`, then
      `xe-forge-skill upstream patch {{ kernel_name }} --kind upstream --notes "..."`.
   2. **serving-only** -- the winner needs a dispatch by shape, or
{%- if integration_repos %} a change to the op's
      interface or call sites. Clone each repository that has to change --
      `upstream clone {{ kernel_repo }}` and, for the call sites,
      {% for r in integration_repos %}`upstream clone {{ r }} --name <short-name>`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} --
      make the edits that wire the winner in, then
      `upstream patch {{ kernel_name }} --kind serving-only --notes "..."`.
{%- else %} is otherwise correct but not
      upstreamable as written. (No integration repository was given, so the op's interface and
      call sites cannot change.) Port into `upstream/` as in 1, then
      `upstream patch {{ kernel_name }} --kind serving-only --notes "..."`.
{%- endif %}
      Say in the notes what makes it serving-only.
   3. **none** -- only when neither applies: write no patch, and name the part of the op's
      interface the winner changes and why no repository given here can carry it.

## Output

- `output/upstream.patch` (and `output/upstream-<name>.patch` for each further clone) --
  `git -C <clone> diff`, paths relative to that repository's root, applicable with
  `git apply` / `patch -p1` at its recorded commit. `upstream patch` writes them.
- `output/PORT.txt` -- `kind: upstream|serving-only`, then per repository: base commit, touched
  files; the best trial and its measured speedup; and what was verified:
  - a Python or Triton file: `python -m py_compile` of every touched file, and, when an
    interpreter that imports the repository's package is available, the repository's own
    test for this op (the profile names it) run against the patched sources;
  - C++/SYCL: say `unbuilt` -- rebuilding the provider is the owner's action, not yours.

Report back the paths written and one line saying what was and was not verified.
